g One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8c4238-10c0-5faf-9d3d-a2cb23c62bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236010} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef7d97e-693d-5415-93bb-198bfe795391" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 4+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236325} {:answer/id #uuid "c46025af-992d-510d-bb0a-539ed37cd414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237058} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c45c20-179b-52a5-b8ae-57164d15e035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-e\\le x<4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237386} {:answer/id #uuid "fbda8f5f-a5d4-54ac-ae47-08273295dbed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8c4238-10c0-5faf-9d3d-a2cb23c62bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 4-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236010} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b31bde-fdeb-50b7-bd6f-55caf828f85c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab8e91c5-e813-5f8f-8b1d-e522d448b269" :question/math-academy-id "q-102977" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x) & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x)} & \\ge {e}^{1} \\\\ 4-x & \\ge e \\\\ -x & \\ge e-4 \\\\ x & \\le 4-e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n4-x>0\\,⇒\\,x<4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 4-e,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4-e].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x<4,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,4-e]\\cap (-\\infty ,4)=(-\\infty ,4-e]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (-\\infty ,4-e],$ which can also be written as $x\\le 4-e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2020696f-a58a-5bea-9042-7644a64564bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541489} {:answer/id #uuid "2f955151-5811-59d2-8a3a-8e38ffc5da58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}<x<7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541516} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7b2476-f5c3-533e-b71b-3b272d7d38bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541639} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c82291-5bcc-5669-952a-35cb81cb2451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541844} {:answer/id #uuid "dac08641-2e13-5838-9a68-0bb5101691ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dac08641-2e13-5838-9a68-0bb5101691ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541889} :answer-field/id #uuid "e453b4f4-6b05-567d-8a7e-2835ff4071a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9adad641-ba08-51a7-99da-8781356ee913" :question/math-academy-id "q-102984" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)>\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-7).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)} & >{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-7)} \\\\ 2-x & >x-7 \\\\ -2x & >-9 \\\\ x & <\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2 \\\\ x-7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{9}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{2}).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<2$ and $x>7,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(7,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{2})\\cap (-\\infty ,2)\\cap (7,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542140 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d892d4b-1f41-59fa-8ad3-d3472fa4e6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541643} {:answer/id #uuid "770500f6-2d7b-540a-b09e-bb29a81643a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}<x<\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541667} {:answer/id #uuid "a32c9ddf-299e-58be-bd3f-212dafb64901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541761} {:answer/id #uuid "a863da24-b3b9-5ace-b9ec-e3e2fd599ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}<x<\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541769} {:answer/id #uuid "d7541595-2650-5a3c-a576-f7811636ecbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{7}<x<\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a863da24-b3b9-5ace-b9ec-e3e2fd599ad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}<x<\\frac{6}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541769} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7f0155d-d662-520e-9a57-f5d8914eb1be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43fb940b-f6bb-5ab7-9419-983a08453584" :question/math-academy-id "q-102985" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-1)<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4x).$" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-1)} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4x)} \\\\ 3x-1 & <5-4x \\\\ 7x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5-4x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{6}{7},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{6}{7}).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x<\\frac{5}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}),$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{6}{7})\\cap (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})=(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{6}{7})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{6}{7}),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{1}{3}<x<\\frac{6}{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91bc0fe5-d471-5c17-a786-dd9129e58e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{10}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bc0fe5-d471-5c17-a786-dd9129e58e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{10}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541722} :answer-field/id #uuid "31666f62-adec-5321-b621-e5a73bb33869" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eafb00a-44c3-51b3-ae6e-6cc24538d944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eafb00a-44c3-51b3-ae6e-6cc24538d944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541485} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa05abfc-ee3b-5acf-886a-026ce4b11282" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a424e3b7-6a84-5fcd-baa0-a03ad7cec111" :question/math-academy-id "q-276949" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)-6>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)-6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4) & >6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4) & >10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)} & >{e}^{10} \\\\ x+4 & >{e}^{10} \\\\ x & >{e}^{10}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>{e}^{10}-4,$ which can be expressed as $({e}^{10}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n({e}^{10}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=({e}^{10}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in ({e}^{10}-4,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>{e}^{10}-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542173 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1770f611-9506-5c65-badc-9ba567423390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541471}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1770f611-9506-5c65-badc-9ba567423390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541471} :answer-field/id #uuid "12d10a1f-799d-5ce9-9008-249ea9bc58c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541974} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3da09dd-8dd6-5605-be7f-0b87e6ec4f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{\\frac{2}{5}}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3da09dd-8dd6-5605-be7f-0b87e6ec4f86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2^{\\frac{2}{5}}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541791} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e07d3f9-6517-533a-8691-42014e926a21" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96832f47-df31-501c-8b04-84adf76c5b92" :question/math-academy-id "q-278513" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2 & >0 \\\\ 5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >2 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)} & >{2}^{2/5} \\\\ x+4 & >\\sqrt[5]{4} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[5]{4}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ),\\text{ which can also be written as }x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723e99d5-0416-531b-b595-4c723239e75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723e99d5-0416-531b-b595-4c723239e75b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\le" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541654} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e755fb-6efa-5f5a-9c85-ee31e2f45915" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f29c2996-fc29-51ba-b7a6-1cefba71fc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f29c2996-fc29-51ba-b7a6-1cefba71fc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541934} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f0cf304-f03b-5af7-9dc8-5eb21f7073db" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdce4bca-4c1e-5ec1-bb2d-2c86adcdbc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdce4bca-4c1e-5ec1-bb2d-2c86adcdbc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541857} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15699de-1abc-5f8c-a730-5e1a1a14dc16" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adae2d29-07f7-5943-8df9-e670e02b37d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-278632" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8).$\n\n{{field-1}}$<\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8)} \\\\ 3x-4 & \\le x+8 \\\\ 2x & \\le 12 \\\\ x & \\le 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x+8 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 6,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{4}{3}$ and $x>-8,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )$ and $(-8,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6]\\cap (\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(\\frac{4}{3},6]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{4}{3},6],$ which can also be written as $\\frac{4}{3}<x\\le 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07bac355-b88f-5b7d-9809-b280d5116526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07bac355-b88f-5b7d-9809-b280d5116526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541437} :answer-field/id #uuid "465d8139-eff1-5a62-b741-0958ddc43158" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f76616c-2327-566a-9ae8-01e2ede0a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f76616c-2327-566a-9ae8-01e2ede0a56b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541554} :answer-field/id #uuid "615ffce8-4d1d-5d35-af0c-b2aca3ff273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a94e199-2676-5231-8dfa-fab7d29de94f" :question/math-academy-id "q-278633" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)-1\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)-1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)}\\cdot {10}^{-1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ (2x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} & \\le x+3 \\\\ 2x-7 & \\le 10x+30 \\\\ -8x & \\le 37 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{37}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ x+3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge -\\frac{37}{8},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{37}{8},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{7}{2}$ and $x>-3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )$ and $(-3,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{37}{8},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "106dce79-251c-52b2-adc7-9fd2b59b9b83" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving Two Logarithmic Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52b9d06-0519-55ac-98ef-015eff6b5566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52b9d06-0519-55ac-98ef-015eff6b5566" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293582} :answer-field/id #uuid "4091b4ab-1606-50ce-92ea-0980c68d9f6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e95aa532-bc97-5306-90f9-13b52cf08c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e95aa532-bc97-5306-90f9-13b52cf08c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293841} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9efe90-3c81-5aaa-9c84-50fe0a47927a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7515ee0f-3bac-5c64-8ed4-d41d3caac6d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-278753" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)}\\cdot {e}^{-1} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)} \\\\ (2x+4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{e} & <x-2 \\\\ 2x+4 & <xe-2e \\\\ 4+2e & <xe-2x \\\\ 4+2e & <x(e-2) \\\\ x & >\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+4 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-2 \\\\ x-2 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-2$ and $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(-2,\\infty )$ and $(2,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\\cap (-2,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{4+2e}{e-2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\nx>\\frac{4+2e}{e-2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c6185ac-3ff7-5ad9-8a7a-c63bebc17ec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541579} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f7a9d3-6a06-59d9-ace8-7b473aee0897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541805} {:answer/id #uuid "c26870c9-271c-5ec8-ba0d-55e750e2893c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<6+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541831} {:answer/id #uuid "ceace2e3-3334-557a-a6f8-644ee4f2d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541859} {:answer/id #uuid "d840e75c-9bea-5129-8361-3aaab6aed04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0<x<6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceace2e3-3334-557a-a6f8-644ee4f2d6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541859} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6b5db-0c42-53f8-93d9-a05a77c02160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e045f62d-3ea7-50f9-adfb-812aba109913" :question/math-academy-id "q-87744" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)-3<2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)-3 & <2 \\\\ 10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6) & <5 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-6) & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)} & <{2}^{1/2} \\\\ x-6 & <\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & <6+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-6>0\\,⇒\\,x>6\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<6+\\sqrt{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2})\\cap (6,\\infty )=(6,6+\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,6+\\sqrt{2}),$ which can also be written as $6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d522133e-f7ca-5cbb-8a42-8377a685b263" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality Involving One Logarithmic Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47dfb872-e75b-5f9a-b45e-fbda4b8838ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x<81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541572} {:answer/id #uuid "7045c8aa-5d5b-5ee3-9e2f-bf220567c38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<x<82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541649} {:answer/id #uuid "84e6302e-5afe-52b3-aec8-107a6a770214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1<x<82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541692} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6b1555-ce02-5e87-a301-c4190c7b7287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2<x<81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541747} {:answer/id #uuid "de6a8700-2d0e-527a-9eef-a3846087e808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7045c8aa-5d5b-5ee3-9e2f-bf220567c38d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1<x<82" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541649} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf8c253d-591c-537b-af74-0a69ba582f86" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e07a88-c6e0-5488-ba80-e3ecff483738" :question/math-academy-id "q-88012" :question/problem "Solve the inequality $3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5<7.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5 & <7 \\\\ 3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <12 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $3.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)} & <{3}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <81 \\\\ x & <82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1>0\\,⇒\\,x>1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<82,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,82).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,82)\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,82)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (1,82),$ which can also be written as $1<x<82.$"}]}
